<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<definitions xmlns="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL"
             xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
             xmlns:flowable="http://flowable.org/bpmn"
             xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI"
             xmlns:omgdc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC"
             xmlns:omgdi="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI"
             typeLanguage="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
             expressionLanguage="http://www.w3.org/1999/XPath"
             targetNamespace="http://inspector.io/demo">

  <!-- issue #359 / RETRYING-RISK-LANE.md §7.2 finding G12: the ONE seed process in the
       harness that fails TRANSIENTLY. Every other fixture (error-zoo, ACME) fails
       PERMANENTLY by construction — correct for DLQ/triage testing, but it means the R2
       self-heal risk lane's SELF_HEAL_LIKELY/SELF_HEAL_MIXED paths (and the §7.2 gate
       check itself) have no end-to-end path anywhere: not dev, not CI, not the demo
       (docs/reviews/R2-SELFHEAL-BASELINE-2026-08.md: pilot history shows zero self-heals,
       structurally, because the demo's own seeds cannot self-heal).

       SHAPE (validate-bpmn "counter- or clock-driven service task"): CLOCK-driven.
       flowable:expression="${healed eq true or selfHealGhost.total gt 0}" — `healed` starts
       undefined (falsy: EL `eq` against null never throws), so EL's short-circuit OR falls
       through to `selfHealGhost.total` — an unresolvable identifier, PropertyNotFoundException,
       the SAME organic-dead-letter mechanism as error-zoo-missing-property.bpmn20.xml (proven
       live against flowable-rest 6.8, 2026-08-04: the exceptionMessage is the FULL literal
       expression text, "Unknown property used in expression: ${...}" — exactly what lets a
       run-unique identifier name keep this fixture's error signature isolated from every other
       seed, the same token trick IncidentLedgerArcIT already relies on for #261).

       A PARALLEL fork at start routes a second token to a timer INTERMEDIATE CATCH event
       (duration = the `healDelay` process variable, an ISO-8601 duration, REQUIRED at
       instance start or the process cannot even start); when it fires, `heal` sets
       `healed=true`. The catch-event timer job is created in the START transaction — which
       COMMITS — so the clock is armed no matter what the failing task does. ⚠ CORRECTED
       2026-08-07 (was FALSE as shipped): the original shape used a non-interrupting
       BOUNDARY timer on the failing task itself. For an async task every attempt transaction
       ROLLS BACK on failure — taking the just-created boundary-timer job with it — so the
       timer never survived to fire and the standalone fixture could never self-heal (proven
       live on engine-a: zero `healTimer` jobs engine-wide while instances retried; the exact
       same same-transaction rollback trap this comment already documented for counters).
       The ITs never caught it BY DESIGN: they pin `healDelay=P1D` and drive `healed` over
       REST, deliberately bypassing the timer path. Once `healed` is true, the OR
       short-circuits and `selfHealGhost.total` is never evaluated again — the failing
       task's very next retry attempt succeeds outright. Real engine-driven self-heal: zero
       custom beans (flowable-rest has none), zero engine mutation performed by any test or
       operator actor, zero inspector corrective-action audit row (so it can never be flagged
       as confounded by RETRYING-RISK-LANE.md §3.3's audit-side scan — this is what makes it a
       GENUINE unconfounded self-heal observation, not a operator-retry-shaped one).

       R30/PT5S (extension ELEMENT — the attribute form is silently ignored, validate-bpmn
       §2) is a deliberately LONG retry cascade so a short `healDelay` reliably outlasts the
       race for the STANDALONE dev/demo instance seed.sh starts. Integration tests instead
       drive `healed` directly over REST at a moment they choose (Awaitility-bound on real
       observed engine state, never a nominal-duration race) and pin `healDelay` to "P1D" so
       the boundary timer never interferes — see SelfHealSeed, which deploys TWO run-unique
       copies of this template (a run-unique identifier substituted in place of
       `selfHealGhost` in both): a HEAL copy keeping this long cascade, and an ESCALATE copy
       whose cascade is substituted for a short, wide one. The two spell outcomes pull in
       opposite directions — a heal spell must still be RETRYING when the test heals it,
       an escalation spell must genuinely EXHAUST inside an Awaitility bound — so one shared
       cascade cannot serve both; that is why the substitution point exists, and why the
       literal above is what SelfHealSeed matches on (it fails loudly if this is edited).

       MEASURED on flowable-6 (2026-08-04): the async executor's timer-job acquire poll adds
       ~9-10s to EVERY retry regardless of the nominal interval, so this cascade is worth
       minutes of retrying, and the demo instance's own `healDelay` should be read against
       that effective rate, not the nominal one.

       Deploy this ALONGSIDE demo-self-healing-baseline.bpmn20.xml, which shares the EXACT
       same `selfHealGhost` identifier (same signature) but fails FAST and PERMANENTLY: a
       spell can only be judged SELF_HEALED/ESCALATED while the class's fleet-wide total
       stays non-zero between samples (RETRYING-RISK-LANE.md §3.1 — the ledger writes an
       occurrence row only for a "live" group, `IncidentLedgerService#ingest`), so a
       standing baseline dead-letter is what keeps the class observable across a transient
       job's own retrying → healed/zero transition instead of the class vanishing from the
       ledger entirely the instant nothing is currently failing.

       Gated 6.8+ by seed.sh (boundary-timer + extension-element retry-cycle parity with the
       rest of the 6.8+-gated fixture set; never deployed to the legacy 6.3.1 profile). -->
  <process id="demoSelfHealing" name="Demo Self-Healing (transient failure)" isExecutable="true">
    <startEvent id="start"/>
    <sequenceFlow id="flow1" sourceRef="start" targetRef="fork"/>
    <parallelGateway id="fork"/>
    <sequenceFlow id="flowAttempt" sourceRef="fork" targetRef="attempt"/>
    <serviceTask id="attempt" name="Transient attempt"
                 flowable:async="true"
                 flowable:expression="${healed eq true or selfHealGhost.total gt 0}">
      <extensionElements>
        <flowable:failedJobRetryTimeCycle>R30/PT5S</flowable:failedJobRetryTimeCycle>
      </extensionElements>
    </serviceTask>
    <sequenceFlow id="flowClock" sourceRef="fork" targetRef="healClock"/>
    <intermediateCatchEvent id="healClock">
      <timerEventDefinition>
        <timeDuration>${healDelay}</timeDuration>
      </timerEventDefinition>
    </intermediateCatchEvent>
    <sequenceFlow id="flowHeal" sourceRef="healClock" targetRef="heal"/>
    <serviceTask id="heal" name="Engine-driven heal (armed at start, commits — no operator, no audit row)"
                 flowable:expression="${execution.setVariable('healed', true)}"/>
    <sequenceFlow id="flowHealEnd" sourceRef="heal" targetRef="healedEnd"/>
    <endEvent id="healedEnd"/>
    <sequenceFlow id="flow2" sourceRef="attempt" targetRef="end"/>
    <!-- TERMINATE, not a plain end: when the attempt succeeds because `healed` was set
         EXTERNALLY (the ITs' REST-driven path, healDelay pinned to P1D), the healClock
         token would otherwise keep the instance alive until that timer fires. Terminate
         reaps it; for the organic timer-driven path both tokens have already ended and
         this is behavior-neutral. -->
    <endEvent id="end">
      <terminateEventDefinition/>
    </endEvent>
  </process>

  <bpmndi:BPMNDiagram id="BPMNDiagram_demoSelfHealing">
    <bpmndi:BPMNPlane id="BPMNPlane_demoSelfHealing" bpmnElement="demoSelfHealing">
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="BPMNShape_start" bpmnElement="start">
        <omgdc:Bounds x="100" y="100" width="30" height="30"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="BPMNShape_attempt" bpmnElement="attempt">
        <omgdc:Bounds x="180" y="85" width="100" height="60"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="BPMNShape_end" bpmnElement="end">
        <omgdc:Bounds x="330" y="101" width="28" height="28"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="BPMNShape_fork" bpmnElement="fork">
        <omgdc:Bounds x="140" y="95" width="40" height="40"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="BPMNShape_healClock" bpmnElement="healClock">
        <omgdc:Bounds x="145" y="215" width="30" height="30"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="BPMNShape_heal" bpmnElement="heal">
        <omgdc:Bounds x="200" y="200" width="100" height="60"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="BPMNShape_healedEnd" bpmnElement="healedEnd">
        <omgdc:Bounds x="350" y="215" width="28" height="28"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="BPMNEdge_flow1" bpmnElement="flow1">
        <omgdi:waypoint x="130" y="115"/>
        <omgdi:waypoint x="140" y="115"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="BPMNEdge_flowAttempt" bpmnElement="flowAttempt">
        <omgdi:waypoint x="180" y="115"/>
        <omgdi:waypoint x="180" y="115"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="BPMNEdge_flowClock" bpmnElement="flowClock">
        <omgdi:waypoint x="160" y="135"/>
        <omgdi:waypoint x="160" y="215"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="BPMNEdge_flow2" bpmnElement="flow2">
        <omgdi:waypoint x="280" y="115"/>
        <omgdi:waypoint x="330" y="115"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="BPMNEdge_flowHeal" bpmnElement="flowHeal">
        <omgdi:waypoint x="175" y="230"/>
        <omgdi:waypoint x="200" y="230"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id="BPMNEdge_flowHealEnd" bpmnElement="flowHealEnd">
        <omgdi:waypoint x="300" y="230"/>
        <omgdi:waypoint x="350" y="229"/>
      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>
    </bpmndi:BPMNPlane>
  </bpmndi:BPMNDiagram>
</definitions>
